{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1392 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b0ec9510359434fa95ac57f4d8114ae9c5732c95f9d24fcb5c04cc424b6efbaf" :edb/db-before-t 1391 :edb/db-after-t 1392 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534704 50 #inst "2026-10-08T04:38:21.121Z" 13194139534704 true] [17592186128862 1100 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534704 true] [17592186128862 1100 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534704 false] [17592186128862 1145 "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534704 true] [17592186128862 1145 "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n1. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n2. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534704 false] [17592186128863 1100 "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534704 true] [17592186128863 1100 "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534704 false] [17592186128863 1145 "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534704 true] [17592186128863 1145 "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n1. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n2. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534704 false] [17592186128864 1100 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534704 true] [17592186128864 1100 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534704 false] [17592186128864 1145 "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534704 true] [17592186128864 1145 "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534704 false] [17592186128930 1100 "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A$, given below?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534704 false] [17592186128930 1100 "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534704 true] [17592186128930 1145 "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry.\n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is\n\n$$\n-1+(-4)+8=3\n$$" 13194139534704 false] [17592186128930 1145 "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-1+(-4)+8=3.$" 13194139534704 true] [17592186129048 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304439 13194139534704 true] [17592186129048 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304446 13194139534704 true] [17592186129048 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304453 13194139534704 true] [17592186129048 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304501 13194139534704 true] [17592186129048 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304508 13194139534704 true] [17592186129049 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304425 13194139534704 true] [17592186129049 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304432 13194139534704 true] [17592186129049 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304480 13194139534704 true] [17592186129049 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304494 13194139534704 true] [17592186129050 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304460 13194139534704 true] [17592186129050 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304487 13194139534704 true] [17592186129050 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304529 13194139534704 true] [17592186129111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304411 13194139534704 true] [17592186129111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304418 13194139534704 true] [17592186129111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304473 13194139534704 true] [17592186129111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304515 13194139534704 true] [17592186129111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304522 13194139534704 true] [17592186129709 1017 #edb/ref 17592186128048 13194139534704 false] [17592186129709 1017 #edb/ref 17592186304467 13194139534704 true] [17592186129709 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534704 true] [17592186129709 1100 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\n\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534704 false] [17592186129709 1100 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534704 true] [17592186129709 1145 "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{21}$ and ${a}_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form." 13194139534704 true] [17592186129710 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534704 true] [17592186129710 1100 "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534704 true] [17592186129710 1100 "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534704 false] [17592186129710 1145 "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ and $C$ are in row echelon form." 13194139534704 true] [17592186129716 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534704 true] [17592186129716 1100 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\n\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534704 false] [17592186129716 1100 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534704 true] [17592186129716 1145 "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{22}$, ${a}_{32}$ and ${a}_{33}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form." 13194139534704 true] [17592186304411 1017 #edb/ref 17592186304412 13194139534704 true] [17592186304411 1089 "q-129204" 13194139534704 true] [17592186304411 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534704 true] [17592186304411 1100 "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534704 true] [17592186304411 1113 #uuid "61eee372-013c-582e-950f-3d35cc24344c" 13194139534704 true] [17592186304411 1145 "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd rows of $A$ are $-3$, $5$, and $2$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-3+5+2=4.$" 13194139